<Guide
  title="How to redact a PDF so the text is actually gone"
  metaTitle="How to redact a PDF properly — and check that it worked"
  description="Drawing a black box over text in a PDF does not remove it; the words can be copied straight back out. Here is what actually removes text, which methods only look like they do, and how to verify a finished file in under a minute."
  path="/guides/redact-a-pdf-properly"
  kicker="Guide"
  standfirst="Every published redaction failure has the same cause, and it is not carelessness — it is a reasonable assumption about how PDFs work that happens to be wrong."
  updated="2026-08-30"
  toc={toc}
  related={related}
>
  <h2 id="what-goes-wrong">Why a black box does not redact anything</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    A PDF page is not a picture. It is a list of drawing instructions that a reader executes in
    order, and the two instructions that matter here are independent of each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first says <strong>write these words at this position</strong>. The second says
    <strong>fill this rectangle in black</strong>. The reader carries them out in sequence, the
    rectangle lands on top, and on screen the words are gone. The instruction that writes the words
    is still in the file, exactly as it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anything that reads the instructions rather than looking at the picture therefore sees the
    original text: select-all and copy, any text-extraction library, the search index of whatever
    system the file is later filed in. This is why redaction failures are usually discovered by
    accident, by someone who pasted a document into an email and watched the blacked-out names
    reappear.
  </p>
  <div class="callout bad">
    <b>The rule</b>
    <p>
      Covering is not removing. If your method adds something to the page, the text is still there.
      Redaction has to take something away.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="methods-that-fail">Methods that look right and are not</h2>
  <p>
    All of these produce a document that looks correctly redacted at every stage of a normal review,
    including on the reviewer's screen and on paper.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>What people do</th><th>What the file ends up containing</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Draw a filled rectangle or use a drawing/markup tool</td>
          <td>The original text, plus a rectangle drawn on top of it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Use the highlighter set to black</td>
          <td>The original text, with a black highlight annotation over it. Often the annotation can simply be deleted.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Change the font colour to black on a black background, or to white</td>
          <td>The original text, in a colour nobody can see. Copying it recovers everything.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cover it in Word, then export to PDF</td>
          <td>Depends entirely on the shape used. A drawn shape leaves the text; deleting the text and typing <code>[REDACTED]</code> does not.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Crop the page so the sensitive part is off the edge</td>
          <td>Everything. Cropping changes the visible window, not the stored content, and it is reversible.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Paste a black image over the area</td>
          <td>The original text, underneath an image.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="print-to-pdf">The print-to-PDF myth</h2>
  <p>
    "Print to PDF flattens everything" is the most confidently repeated piece of bad advice in this
    area. It is sometimes true and sometimes not, which is worse than being simply wrong, because
    it works often enough that people trust it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most print-to-PDF drivers rebuild the page from the same drawing instructions rather than from
    an image of it. Text that goes in as text usually comes out as text — including text that had a
    black rectangle sitting on top of it. What actually changes is the file's <em>history</em>:
    earlier revisions, form field values and annotations are commonly dropped, which is genuinely
    useful for other kinds of leak. Just not for this one.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <b>If you take one thing from this page</b>
    <p>
      Never treat a conversion step as a redaction step. Whatever you do, the final file is the one
      that has to be checked.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-works">What actually removes text</h2>
  <p>There are only three approaches that end with the words no longer in the file.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Delete the text at the source, before the PDF exists.</strong> Edit the original
      document, remove the sensitive words, put a placeholder in their place, and export a fresh
      PDF. This is the most reliable method available to anyone, and it needs no special software.
      Its limitation is that you must still hold the source file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use a dedicated redaction feature, not a drawing tool.</strong> Professional PDF
      editors distinguish between marking an area and applying the redaction — the second step is
      the one that deletes content, and it is a separate, deliberate action. If the feature you are
      using is in the same menu as shapes, arrows and highlighters, it is a drawing tool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Turn the affected pages into images.</strong> Render the page to a picture and rebuild
      the PDF from it. Nothing can be recovered because no text objects remain. The cost is real:
      those pages stop being selectable, searchable and accessible to a screen reader, and the file
      grows. It is the right trade when certainty matters more than convenience, and it is what
      ZeroLeak's cleaner does to affected pages.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Whichever you choose, the sensitive text has to be gone before the black box goes on. A box on
    top of nothing is a fine visual marker; a box on top of text is a lie.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify">How to verify, in under a minute</h2>
  <p>
    Verification is not optional, because every failure mode above is invisible in a normal review.
    Two checks, in increasing order of thoroughness:
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Select and copy.</strong> Open the finished PDF, select the whole page (Ctrl+A or
      Cmd+A), copy, and paste into a plain text editor. If redacted words appear, you have your
      answer immediately. This catches the common cases and takes ten seconds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scan the structure.</strong> Copying only reveals what a viewer chooses to expose. It
      will not show you an invisible text layer, content pushed off the page, an earlier revision
      of the document still stored inside the file, or the author's name in the metadata. A
      structural scan reads the file itself. <a href="/">ZeroLeak</a> does this in your browser —
      the document is not uploaded anywhere — and shows you the exact text it was able to recover
      from underneath each box.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout good">
    <b>Check the file you are about to send</b>
    <p>
      Not the draft, not the version before the last edit. Redaction survives right up until
      somebody re-exports the document and quietly reintroduces the text layer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="beyond-boxes">The leaks that are not on the page at all</h2>
  <p>
    A document can be perfectly redacted and still give away more than the redaction hid. In
    practice these are found more often than failed boxes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Author and title metadata.</strong> Enough to identify the individual who prepared an anonymously filed document, and a working title that names the matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Earlier revisions.</strong> A PDF saved incrementally keeps every previous state of itself, including the paragraph you deleted before sending.</li>
    <li><strong>Embedded files.</strong> Whole spreadsheets stapled inside a report, invisible in most readers.</li>
    <li><strong>Review comments.</strong> Candid internal notes, tagged with the name of whoever wrote them.</li>
    <li><strong>Form field values.</strong> Stored separately from the page you see, and sometimes retaining what was there before.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/guides/hidden-content-in-pdfs">A field guide to all of them is here</a>, and
    <a href="/guides/remove-pdf-metadata">removing metadata has its own page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="process">A process that survives a bad day</h2>
  <p>
    Individual care does not scale and does not survive deadlines. What works is making the check
    a step rather than a habit:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Redact in the source document</strong> wherever you still have it, and export fresh.</li>
    <li><strong>Never let a conversion count as a redaction.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Scan the final artefact</strong>, after every re-export, as the last step before release.</li>
    <li><strong>Strip metadata at the same moment</strong>, so it cannot be forgotten separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Make the check someone's job, not everyone's</strong>, or automate it so it is nobody's.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The organisations this has happened to were not careless. They reviewed the document, looked at
    the black boxes, and saw exactly what they expected to see. The only thing that catches this is
    a check that reads the file instead of looking at it.
  </p>
</Guide>
